<http://data.silknow.org/object/ebeb8ab0-19bd-35ad-bbff-7f5f79c91a69/observation/2>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Wide-brimmed hats with shallow crowns were popular for women in the mid 18th century. They were often made in a range of materials such as straw, openwork, paper and ribbon.  This example shows the use of feathers of common origin, such as those from cocks or guinea fowl, dyed in a variety of colours for a vivid effect. Such hats were worn as fashionable daywear with short jackets and petticoats. They demonstrate the 18th-century trend for taking items traditionally associated with working-class dress and transforming them into fashionable styles."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/ebeb8ab0-19bd-35ad-bbff-7f5f79c91a69> .

